            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"DIVERGENT\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The consensus primarily identifies lamp oil as a symbol of the Holy Spirit and His empowering, sustaining grace, while acknowledging secondary interpretations such as spiritual readiness, personal faith, the Word of God, or good works.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The book defines lamp oil as the tested fruit of the Spirit and the evidence of faithful obedience, which is burned to provide a visible example (good works).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"The Holy Spirit.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The fruit of the Spirit; evidence of faithful obedience.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"CONTRADICTS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"DIFFERENT_REFERENT\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus views lamp oil primarily as the divine source of power (the Holy Spirit), whereas the book views it as the human response or product resulting from that source (the fruit of the Spirit and faithful obedience).\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"The book explicitly rejects the direct identification of oil as the Holy Spirit (as noted in its contrast with the 'Commonly taught' view), choosing instead to identify it as the 'fruit' of the Spirit and obedience. It materially shifts the symbol from the source (the Spirit) to the product (works/obedience).\",\n  \"rationale\": \"By contrasting its definition with the common teaching that oil is the Holy Spirit, the book establishes a divergent core identification. Although the book's definition aligns with some recognized alternative minority views (such as good works or obedience), it stands in material contradiction to the primary consensus referent.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Zechariah 4:6 explicitly interprets the vision of the olive trees and the golden oil feeding the lampstand with the declaration, 'Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts,' providing strong biblical warrant for directly identifying the oil with the Holy Spirit rather than human obedience.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
